WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.836
-My next guest is an Oscar- and
Golden Globe-winning director.

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:06.539
He's also produced a new
National Geographic special

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.608
called "Secrets of the Whales,"

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.843
which premieres tomorrow
on Disney+.

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:14.246
Here is James Cameron!

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:18.284
Yeah! Hey, that's what
I'm talking about.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.086
-Hey.
-It is so good to see you,

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:21.621
buddy. Welcome back
to "The Tonight Show."

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.689
And thank you for coming on.

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:27.093
-It's so great to be
sort of on the show virtually.

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.062
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, I know.
Exactly, right?

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:32.098
You must be living in kind of
a virtual life now

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.435
with the "Avatar 2" and "3,"
right? Everything's virtual.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:39.238
-Yeah, you're actually visiting
me in my cutting room right now.

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:41.574
I'm down here in
Wellington, New Zealand.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:47.046
-You're in the bunker there
working on "Avatar 2" and "3."

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.682
I can't wait.
You know I'm fan of yours.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.118
And "Avatar" was unlike anything

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:54.353
I've ever experienced
in the movies.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:55.688
I loved it so much.

00:00:55.688 --> 00:01:00.827
And how do you top "Avatar"
with "2" and "3"?

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.061
-I cry.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:03.563
I just sit here at my desk.
-[ Laughs ] Yes.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.532
-And I cry all day long,
because there's no way.

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:07.933
-Yeah, of course.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.904
-And it's just too much
pressure, dude, you know?

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:15.040
No, look, we just
try to be kind of true

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.843
to what people loved
about the first film

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:20.613
and take it into, you know,
whole new areas,

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:23.816
like a lot of our scenes
are in the ocean.

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:25.618
-Oh, really?
-And so I'm combining things

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:28.353
I love, like making "Avatar"
and going into the ocean.

00:01:28.353 --> 00:01:29.855
I love all that stuff.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.657
-I was just reading something
where it said

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:35.227
that "Avatar" is the highest
grossing film of all time,

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:39.565
and then it got beat out
by "Avengers,"

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:42.101
and that was
the #1 highest grossing,

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:46.072
but then "Avatar" became again
the highest grossing film.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:49.843
Do you care about that stuff,
or you don't care?

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.445
-Well, look, I mean, everybody
makes a big deal out of it,

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.913
but the truth is

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:58.284
what we really need to focus on
is getting back to theaters.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.588
And, you know, hopefully, we can
still have films like that,

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:05.257
like "Endgame" and "Avatar," the
big Marvel movies and all that

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:09.062
that are able to make, you know,
$1 billion or $2 billion.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:13.766
Let's pray that the theaters
are there after this pandemic

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.536
and after this shift
toward streaming --

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:17.837
not that I have anything
against streaming.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.772
There's great writing,
great shows.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.074
But let's remember
that the movie theater

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:24.310
is kind of a sacred experience
for all of us.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.012
And let's get back out there
when it's safe to do so.

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:29.849
-Yeah, let's get to -- I agree.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.518
I think people will go.

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:35.888
So, now this project
that you're working on,

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:37.823
"The Secrets of the Whales."
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:37.823 --> 00:02:41.527
-You get so up-close
and personal with --

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:43.262
It's the most beautiful shots.

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:47.299
I mean, obviously, if anyone
ever saw a whale ever, it is --

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:50.102
You talk about it for months,
I mean, maybe years.

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:52.471
You go, "I saw a whale!" And --

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:54.407
No, you've never seen a whale
like this.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.476
This is unbelievable.
-Yeah.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:59.612
-What is the feeling you want
people to get from this special?

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.547
-Yeah, just want to --
Full disclosure,

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:04.817
I wasn't in the water
with the whales on this show.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.819
I've done that other times.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.687
-Right.
-But we had Brian Skerry

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.757
and some of the top, you know,
underwater photographers

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.259
at National Geographic.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.394
And I'm an explorer
in residence there,

00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:18.698
which is kind of an oxymoron.
But I know all those guys.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.134
So we cooked this thing up
about three years ago.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:25.504
It's taken three years to shoot,
24 locations around the world.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:28.107
The idea was what are we going
to show about whales

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:31.444
that hasn't been seen
and that hasn't been known?

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:35.415
So the goal was find out
new things, see new things,

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.050
also do amazingly
beautiful photography.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.153
But we found out that they --
They have culture.

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:43.455
They have emotion. They grieve.

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:45.824
They have joy. They play.

00:03:45.824 --> 00:03:49.628
They have complex language.
They have music.

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:52.464
They have family bonds.
They teach each other.

00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:55.901
They have taught culture
that's handed down.

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:58.604
And they're very, very complex
and amazing animals.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:01.307
They're not just big
and beautiful and kind of,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.342
you know, mysterious
and awe-inspiring.

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.611
They're people.
They're basically people.

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:09.282
I think, if anybody should
get a takeaway from this show,

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.884
it's that whales are people
just like us.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.753
And they're trying
to survive out there.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.889
They're trying to keep
their families fed and healthy,

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:21.126
and hopefully, the show can
create some kind of a bond,

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:23.529
a compassionate bond with them.

00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:24.730
-Yeah.
-'Cause we have to protect them.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.365
We have to help them survive.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:27.833
-Yeah. Good for you. I agree.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:31.137
I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's a very unique moment

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:34.173
captured in "Secrets
of the Whales." Take a look.

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:37.176
-This one female
adult orca came in

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:40.179
and, you know, was sort of
checking me out.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:43.415
♪♪

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:45.418
It was kind of looking at me,
checking out the ray.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.387
It was kind of like, you know,
"Are you going to have that?

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:50.256
'Cause if not,
I'm going to go grab it."

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:52.724
♪♪

00:04:52.724 --> 00:04:56.228
And then just sort of picks it
up and almost poses, man.

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:59.665
She just kind of
lifts her head up.

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:01.767
It was really remarkable.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.804
-Yeah. That's amazing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:07.440
-Isn't that cool?
-That is really cool.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.776
-So, this mother orca says,
"You look skinny.

00:05:10.776 --> 00:05:12.845
You should eat.
Here, have something."

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.347
-Yeah, yeah. "Eat, eat!"
Yeah, of course, exactly.

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:18.350
-"You're not going to eat that?
Alright, I'm taking it back."

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.552
-James Cameron, everybody.

